-- Task & Project Management System: demo dataset (rebuilt 2026-10-13)
-- Runs after 01-init.sql (docker-entrypoint-initdb.d executes every .sql file in this folder in
-- filename order, against an empty data directory).
--
-- A believable portfolio for a presentation, not a random fixture: ten projects at different
-- stages (planning, running, delayed, on hold, completed), each with milestones, dependencies,
-- subtasks, checklists, threaded comments, time logs, approvals (pending and decided), a few
-- files, a project lead, and the matching activity feed, notifications and administrator audit
-- log. Everything is dated relative to the day the file runs (CURRENT_DATE), so the story stays
-- current whenever the database is built: some tasks are overdue, some are due this week, one
-- project is delayed.
--
-- What is deliberately fixed (the e2e suite and the documentation rely on it): the 24 accounts
-- and their usernames, the project codes PRJ-2001 .. PRJ-2010, who owns what, and the teams.
--   * PRJ-2001 (Team A) and PRJ-2009 (Team B) share no users, to test data isolation.
--   * pm.olivia owns PRJ-2001/2003/2007/2010 and is only a VIEWER of PRJ-2008, and is not a member
--     of PRJ-2002 or PRJ-2005; dev.chen is on PRJ-2001 and PRJ-2005; qa.zoe is a VIEWER of PRJ-2010.
--   * contractor.felix is INACTIVE, exemployee.diego SUSPENDED, newuser belongs to nothing.
--   * dev.tomas has a PENDING invitation to PRJ-2001; there are three DECLINED invitations.
--   * No extra custom system role is seeded (an administrator creates those from the UI).
--
-- Rules the data respects, so the database triggers never object: a task's due date is inside
-- its project; assignees are ACTIVE members with ACTIVE accounts; a task is never running or done
-- while a prerequisite is not COMPLETED; a COMPLETED task has only completed subtasks (and a
-- COMPLETED, 100% progress and a completion date); a task in review has one open approval
-- request; a project lead is an ACTIVE Team Leader of the project.
--
-- password_hash below is a real bcrypt hash (cost 10, generated by this app's own PasswordEncoder)
-- of the plaintext "DevPassword123!"; every seeded user shares it. Development/test credentials only.


